1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,623
Adam:

2
00:00:00,623 --> 00:00:03,798
So, Michelle, shall we make a start on our presentation?

3
00:00:03,798 --> 00:00:06,190
We haven’t got that much time left.

4
00:00:06,190 --> 00:00:06,240
Michelle:

5
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:07,192
No, Adam.

6
00:00:07,192 --> 00:00:10,534
But at least we’ve done all the background reading.

7
00:00:10,534 --> 00:00:15,212
I found it really interesting – I’d never even heard of the Laki eruption

8
00:00:15,212 --> 00:00:15,880
before this.

9
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:16,296
Adam:

10
00:00:16,296 --> 00:00:17,129
Me neither.

11
00:00:17,129 --> 00:00:20,460
I suppose 1783 is a long time ago.

12
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:20,838
Michelle:

13
00:00:20,838 --> 00:00:25,380
But it was a huge eruption and it had such devastating consequences.

14
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:25,736
Adam:

15
00:00:25,736 --> 00:00:26,447
I know.

16
00:00:26,447 --> 00:00:30,713
It was great there were so many primary sources to look at.

17
00:00:30,713 --> 00:00:34,980
It really gives you a sense of how catastrophic the volcano was.

18
00:00:34,980 --> 00:00:39,600
People were really trying to make sense of the science for the first time.

19
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:40,002
Michelle:

20
00:00:40,002 --> 00:00:41,210
That’s right.

21
00:00:41,210 --> 00:00:45,636
But what I found more significant was how it impacted directly

22
00:00:45,636 --> 00:00:51,270
and indirectly on political events, as well as having massive social and economic consequences.

23
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:51,618
Adam:

24
00:00:51,618 --> 00:00:52,312
I know.

25
00:00:52,312 --> 00:00:55,440
That should be the main focus of our presentation.

26
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:55,759
Michelle:

27
00:00:55,759 --> 00:00:59,910
The observations made by people at the time were interesting, weren’t they?

28
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:05,351
I mean, they all gave a pretty consistent account of what happened, even if

29
00:01:05,351 --> 00:01:08,460
they didn’t always use the same terminology.

30
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:08,791
Adam:

31
00:01:08,791 --> 00:01:09,121
Yeah.

32
00:01:09,121 --> 00:01:13,420
I was surprised there were so many weather stations established by that time –

33
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:18,050
so, you know, you can see how the weather changed, often by the hour.

34
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:18,466
Michelle:

35
00:01:18,466 --> 00:01:18,882
Right.

36
00:01:18,882 --> 00:01:24,706
Writers at the time talked about the Laki haze to describe the volcanic fog

37
00:01:24,706 --> 00:01:26,370
that spread across Europe.

38
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:31,678
They all realised that this wasn’t the sort of fog they were used to –

39
00:01:31,678 --> 00:01:36,632
and of course this was in pre-industrial times – so they hadn’t experienced sulphur-smelling

40
00:01:36,632 --> 00:01:37,340
fog before.

41
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:37,812
Adam:

42
00:01:37,812 --> 00:01:38,284
No,

43
00:01:38,284 --> 00:01:39,700
that’s true.

44
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:40,022
Michelle:

45
00:01:40,022 --> 00:01:44,530
Reports from the period blamed the haze for an increase in headaches, respiratory issues

46
00:01:44,530 --> 00:01:45,496
and asthma attacks.

47
00:01:45,496 --> 00:01:50,004
And they all describe how it covered the sun and made it look a

48
00:01:50,004 --> 00:01:50,970
strange red colour.

49
00:01:50,970 --> 00:01:51,418
Adam:

50
00:01:51,418 --> 00:01:53,660
Must have been very weird.

51
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:54,051
Michelle:

52
00:01:54,051 --> 00:01:57,959
It’s interesting that Benjamin Franklin wrote about the haze.

53
00:01:57,959 --> 00:01:59,522
Did you read that?

54
00:01:59,522 --> 00:02:03,430
He was the American ambassador in Paris at the time.

55
00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:03,774
Adam:

56
00:02:03,774 --> 00:02:04,119
Yeah.

57
00:02:04,119 --> 00:02:08,941
At first no one realised that the haze was caused by the volcanic eruption

58
00:02:08,941 --> 00:02:09,630
in Iceland.

59
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:10,017
Michelle:

60
00:02:10,017 --> 00:02:13,890
It was Benjamin Franklin who realised that before anyone else.

61
00:02:13,890 --> 00:02:14,170
Adam:

62
00:02:14,170 --> 00:02:16,130
He’s often credited with that, apparently.

63
00:02:16,130 --> 00:02:20,050
But a French naturalist beat him to it – I can’t remember his name.

64
00:02:20,050 --> 00:02:22,010
I’d have to look it up.

65
00:02:22,010 --> 00:02:27,560
Then other naturalists had the same idea – all independently of each other.

66
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:28,027
Michelle:

67
00:02:28,027 --> 00:02:28,962
Oh right.

68
00:02:28,962 --> 00:02:33,634
We should talk about the immediate impact of the eruption,

69
00:02:33,634 --> 00:02:39,240
which was obviously enormous – especially in Iceland, where so many people died.

70
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:39,588
Adam:

71
00:02:39,588 --> 00:02:39,936
Mmm.

72
00:02:39,936 --> 00:02:44,810
You’d expect that – and the fact that the volcanic ash drifted so swiftly –

73
00:02:44,810 --> 00:02:48,640
but not that the effects would go on for so long.

74
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:55,368
Or that two years after the eruption, strange weather events were being reported as far away as North America

75
00:02:55,368 --> 00:02:56,430
and North Africa.

76
00:02:56,430 --> 00:02:56,866
Michelle:

77
00:02:56,866 --> 00:02:57,301
No.

78
00:02:57,301 --> 00:03:00,786
I found all that hard to believe too.

79
00:03:00,786 --> 00:03:06,448
It must have been terrible – and there was nothing anyone could do about

80
00:03:06,448 --> 00:03:12,110
it, even if they knew the ash cloud was coming in their direction.

81
00:03:12,110 --> 00:03:12,480
Michelle:

82
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:17,660
We should run through some of the terrible consequences of the eruption experienced in

83
00:03:17,660 --> 00:03:18,400
different countries.

84
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:20,620
There’s quite a varied range.

85
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:21,069
Adam:

86
00:03:21,069 --> 00:03:25,560
Starting with Iceland, where the impact on farming was devastating.

87
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:25,908
Michelle:

88
00:03:25,908 --> 00:03:26,257
Mmm.

89
00:03:26,257 --> 00:03:31,136
One of the most dramatic things there was the effect on livestock as they

90
00:03:31,136 --> 00:03:32,530
grazed in the fields.

91
00:03:32,530 --> 00:03:37,927
They were poisoned because they ate vegetation that had been contaminated with fluorine as

92
00:03:37,927 --> 00:03:40,240
a result of the volcanic fallout.

93
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:40,622
Adam:

94
00:03:40,622 --> 00:03:41,767
That was horrible.

95
00:03:41,767 --> 00:03:45,965
In Egypt, the bizarre weather patterns led to a severe drought

96
00:03:45,965 --> 00:03:49,400
and as a result the Nile didn’t flood,

97
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:51,690
which meant the crops all failed.

98
00:03:51,690 --> 00:03:52,048
Michelle:

99
00:03:52,048 --> 00:03:57,413
It’s so far from where the eruption happened and yet the famine there led

100
00:03:57,413 --> 00:04:00,274
to more people dying than any other country.

101
00:04:00,274 --> 00:04:02,420
It was worse than the plague.

102
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:02,869
Adam:

103
00:04:02,869 --> 00:04:03,319
OK.

104
00:04:03,319 --> 00:04:09,161
Then in the UK the mortality rate went up a lot – presumably from

105
00:04:09,161 --> 00:04:10,060
respiratory illnesses.

106
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:14,277
According to one report it was about double the usual number

107
00:04:14,277 --> 00:04:19,260
and included an unusually high percentage of people under the age of 25.

108
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:19,654
Michelle:

109
00:04:19,654 --> 00:04:20,048
Mmm.

110
00:04:20,048 --> 00:04:25,564
I think people will be surprised to hear that the weather in the USA

111
00:04:25,564 --> 00:04:27,140
was badly affected too..

112
00:04:27,140 --> 00:04:33,221
George Washington even makes a note in his diary that they were snowbound until

113
00:04:33,221 --> 00:04:34,524
March in Virginia.

114
00:04:34,524 --> 00:04:37,130
That was before he became president.

115
00:04:37,130 --> 00:04:37,528
Adam:

116
00:04:37,528 --> 00:04:37,925
Yes,

117
00:04:37,925 --> 00:04:41,107
and there was ice floating down the Mississippi,

118
00:04:41,107 --> 00:04:42,300
which was unprecedented.

119
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:42,926
Michelle:

120
00:04:42,926 --> 00:04:44,179
Astonishing, really.

121
00:04:44,179 --> 00:04:47,310
Anyway, what do you think …
